description:
A second launch of a single-instance editor forwards its arguments and its current folder to the running copy (lesson 6.01). Write the encoding of that request and the primary's resolution of the files to open:

```cpp
struct Forwarded { std::string cwd; std::vector<std::string> args; };

std::string encodeForward(const Forwarded& f);
std::optional<Forwarded> decodeForward(const std::string& text);
std::string resolvePath(const std::string& cwd, const std::string& path);
std::vector<std::string> filesToOpen(const Forwarded& f);
```

- **encodeForward** writes `cwd`, then each argument, each followed by a zero byte (`'\0'`); **decodeForward** reads them back. Text that is empty or does not end with a zero is malformed.
- **resolvePath** uses Windows rules with `\`: a path starting with a drive (`C:\`) or `\\` (a network path) is already absolute; otherwise it is joined to `cwd`. Then `.` parts are removed and `..` removes the previous part (never going above the drive or the `\\server\share`). The result never ends with `\` unless it is just a drive root (`C:\`).
- **filesToOpen** takes the arguments that are not options (options start with `-` or `/`) and resolves each against `cwd`, keeping their order and skipping exact duplicates of a path already listed (compare ignoring case).

